Let a pressed gesture's command decline the swipe it was matched for.

lisgd fires a pressed gesture repeatedly during a drag, and on each match it
advances that slot's leg start, so what a release gesture is finally measured
over is only the travel since the last pressed fire. It treats a *binding match*
as success, whatever the command did.

That is wrong whenever a pressed binding is conditional. Here the one-finger
resize bindings run touch-resize.sh, which does nothing unless the finger is
near a window boundary -- and it usually is not, because dragging a finger is
also how you scroll. Every such drag still counted, so any release gesture that
began away from a screen edge arrived at touch-up measuring about 10px and was
read as no swipe at all. Close-window never fired once; a verbose capture showed
four attempts in a row arriving as swipe -1, against four no-op resize fires.

So: a command that exits non-zero has matched but declined, and the leg is left
alone to go on growing into whatever gesture it turns out to be. Exiting zero
keeps the old meaning. Anything odd -- system() failing, a signal -- counts as
acted upon, which is also the old behaviour.

Declining still has to advance *something*, or a binding that never acts would
fire on every motion event for the whole drag. Hence xfire[], the point of the
last fire, kept separate from xstart[]: the pressed leg is measured from it, so
the throttle stays one fire per distancethreshold_pressed of travel either way,
while xstart keeps meaning "where the swipe a release gesture will see began".

Applies on top of lisgd-export-gesture-coords.patch and
lisgd-cardinals-before-diagonals.patch.

